    "presentation": "A 66-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes on metformin 1000 mg BID and oral semaglutide (Rybelsus 3 mg) presents with persistent hyperglycemia and unintended weight loss. She discontinued pioglitazone and sitagliptin due to osteoporosis. Despite no GI side effects on Rybelsus, she has lost 5 lbs (now 104 lbs) since starting it one month ago. She reports fasting and post-prandial glucose readings as follows:\n\nFasting glucose >200 mg/dL\nPost-prandial glucose 150–200 mg/dL\n\nHer A1c and weight over the past year:\n\n12 months ago: A1c 7.4%, 109 lbs\n6 months ago: A1c 7.5%, 108 lbs\n1 month ago: A1c 8.1%, 109 lbs\nToday: 104 lbs\n\nBMI is 19.7 today Patient is willing to switch to an injectable GLP-1 receptor agonist to improve blood sugar control while minimizing further weight loss. Would dulaglutide be appropriate?",
    "recommendations": "I'm actually quite concerned that the patient has a pancreatic process (mass, pancreatitis, LADA) that indicates she has decreasing insulin production. Would have recommended work-up for LADA (GAD65 and IA2 antibodies), assessing underlying insulin production (paired glucose with c-peptide), comprehensive metabolic panel, TSH, and urinalysis or serum beta-hydroxybutyrate for ketones, with a low threshold for CT abdomen to evaluate for weight loss and potential pancreatic lesion. Would stop semaglutide and start weight-based basal insulin and continuous glucose monitoring, in addition to carb consistent diet. Given the complexity of this presentation, would place an urgent referral to Endocrinology."
